1 SPECIFY

   How commands name files, on a mounted volume or on the host.

2 File_spec

   A file on a mounted volume is named as on VMS:

     node::device:[directory]name.type;version

   DUA1:[WORK.SUB]REPORT.TXT;3 is version 3 of REPORT.TXT in the
   directory [WORK.SUB] on the volume mounted on DUA1:.  A part left out
   is the default device and directory's (see SET DEFAULT), and a
   version left out is the newest (DELETE and PURGE have their own
   rules).  A device may be a logical name (SYS$LIBRARY:STARTUP.COM), and
   a name or type may hold the wildcards * (any characters) and % (one
   character) where the command allows.  [-] is the directory above,
   [.SUB] one below the default, and [...] in a search every directory
   below.

2 Host_files

   A command decides whether a name is a host file or a volume file by
   these rules:

     - with /HOST after it, it is a host path;
     - a name with a directory in brackets ([DIR]HELLO.MAR), or a device
       or logical name of two or more characters (DUA1:HELLO.MAR,
       SRC:HELLO.MAR), is on a mounted volume;
     - a name with "/" or "\" (or a Windows drive letter) is a host
       path;
     - any other name (HELLO.MAR) is in the default directory when SET
       DEFAULT names a mounted volume, and a host file otherwise.

   DCL makes unquoted text uppercase, so quote a host path to keep its
   case: TYPE "notes/todo.txt".  Relative host paths are from the
   directory govax was started in.
